574
00:18:42,822 --> 00:18:43,751
Same company.

575
00:18:43,931 --> 00:18:45,034
Same salary.

576
00:18:45,214 --> 00:18:47,606
Thirty five years of different defaults.

577
00:18:47,955 --> 00:18:49,279
And here's the twist.

578
00:18:49,459 --> 00:18:51,549
Megan can still change her ending.

579
00:18:51,729 --> 00:18:54,305
At forty, with her highest earning years

580
00:18:54,305 --> 00:18:57,202
ahead, raising her contribution to fifteen or

581
00:18:57,202 --> 00:18:59,843
twenty percent and avoiding big new fixed

582
00:18:59,843 --> 00:19:02,805
costs can dramatically improve her retirement.

583
00:19:02,985 --> 00:19:05,144
The plan works best when started early.

584
00:19:05,324 --> 00:19:07,054
But it works whenever you start.

585
00:19:07,404 --> 00:19:09,250
Now meet a third person.

586
00:19:09,430 --> 00:19:11,576
Rosa is a school bus driver who

587
00:19:11,576 --> 00:19:14,413
earns about forty five thousand dollars a

588
00:19:14,413 --> 00:19:14,759
year.

589
00:19:14,939 --> 00:19:17,349
She doesn't have a big employer match,

590
00:19:17,349 --> 00:19:18,998
so she opens a Roth IRA at

591
00:19:18,998 --> 00:19:21,345
twenty eight and sets up an automatic

592
00:19:21,345 --> 00:19:24,262
contribution of three hundred dollars a month.

593
00:19:24,442 --> 00:19:26,507
She drives the same car for twelve

594
00:19:26,507 --> 00:19:28,815
years and rents a small apartment near

595
00:19:28,815 --> 00:19:29,423
her route.

596
00:19:29,772 --> 00:19:31,584
Every time she gets a raise,

597
00:19:31,584 --> 00:19:33,847
she adds twenty five dollars to her

598
00:19:33,847 --> 00:19:35,206
monthly contribution.

599
00:19:35,386 --> 00:19:36,766
After about ten years,

600
00:19:36,766 --> 00:19:39,653
she's contributing around five hundred fifty a

601
00:19:39,653 --> 00:19:40,030
month.

602
00:19:40,210 --> 00:19:42,941
If her investments average seven percent a

603
00:19:42,941 --> 00:19:45,151
year, by sixty five she could have

604
00:19:45,151 --> 00:19:46,906
close to a million dollars,

605
00:19:46,906 --> 00:19:49,052
mostly tax free under Roth rules,

606
00:19:49,052 --> 00:19:51,978
because her contributions kept rising and she

607
00:19:51,978 --> 00:19:52,888
never stopped.

608
00:19:53,237 --> 00:19:55,896
Rosa's story shows something important.

609
00:19:56,076 --> 00:19:58,224
She didn't earn a big salary.

610
00:19:58,404 --> 00:20:00,378
She didn't pick hot stocks.

611
00:20:00,558 --> 00:20:01,765
She didn't get lucky.

612
00:20:01,945 --> 00:20:05,318
She automated, increased her contributions slowly,

613
00:20:05,318 --> 00:20:07,274
and avoided the big mistakes.

614
00:20:07,454 --> 00:20:09,126
These are illustrations,

615
00:20:09,126 --> 00:20:10,171
not guarantees.

616
00:20:10,351 --> 00:20:12,887
But the behavior behind them is available

617
00:20:12,887 --> 00:20:13,938
to almost anyone.

618
00:20:14,288 --> 00:20:17,360
If you're already saving fifteen percent,

619
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:19,907
have an emergency fund and no high

620
00:20:19,907 --> 00:20:22,230
interest debt, congratulations.

621
00:20:22,410 --> 00:20:23,861
You're ahead of most people.

622
00:20:24,041 --> 00:20:26,312
Your job now is mostly to protect

623
00:20:26,312 --> 00:20:26,932
the plan.

624
00:20:27,112 --> 00:20:28,087
Keep costs low,

625
00:20:28,087 --> 00:20:30,363
rebalance once a year if your funds

626
00:20:30,363 --> 00:20:31,663
don't do it for you,

627
00:20:31,663 --> 00:20:33,613
and resist the urge to tinker.

628
00:20:33,963 --> 00:20:36,386
Then think about raising your savings rate

629
00:20:36,386 --> 00:20:37,655
toward twenty percent,

630
00:20:37,655 --> 00:20:39,962
which can shorten your path to financial

631
00:20:39,962 --> 00:20:41,231
independence by years.

632
00:20:41,411 --> 00:20:43,212
Look at your investment fees,

633
00:20:43,212 --> 00:20:45,384
because a difference of even half a

634
00:20:45,384 --> 00:20:47,681
percent a year compounds into a large

635
00:20:47,681 --> 00:20:48,923
amount over decades.

636
00:20:49,103 --> 00:20:50,923
And make sure your partner,

637
00:20:50,923 --> 00:20:52,002
if you have one,

638
00:20:52,002 --> 00:20:53,688
understands the plan too,

639
00:20:53,688 --> 00:20:56,115
so it survives whatever life brings.

640
00:20:56,465 --> 00:20:58,358
And finally, give yourself credit.

641
00:20:58,538 --> 00:21:01,297
Building wealth on an average salary takes

642
00:21:01,297 --> 00:21:03,925
patience in a world that celebrates fast

643
00:21:03,925 --> 00:21:04,320
money.

644
00:21:04,500 --> 00:21:06,442
Every month you stick with the plan

645
00:21:06,442 --> 00:21:07,274
is a quiet win.

646
00:21:07,454 --> 00:21:09,345
You may never post it on social

647
00:21:09,345 --> 00:21:11,785
media, but your future self will notice.

648
00:21:12,134 --> 00:21:14,295
Let me answer the questions people ask

649
00:21:14,295 --> 00:21:16,285
me most about building wealth on an

650
00:21:16,285 --> 00:21:17,138
average salary.

651
00:21:17,488 --> 00:21:18,324
Question one.

652
00:21:18,504 --> 00:21:20,255
What if I earn less than sixty

653
00:21:20,255 --> 00:21:20,780
thousand?

654
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:22,260
The same plan works.

655
00:21:22,440 --> 00:21:24,971
The percentages matter more than the amounts.

656
00:21:25,151 --> 00:21:27,794
Saving ten percent of forty thousand is

657
00:21:27,794 --> 00:21:29,285
still building wealth,

658
00:21:29,285 --> 00:21:31,724
and the Saver's Credit and Roth IRAs

659
00:21:31,724 --> 00:21:34,706
can be especially valuable at lower incomes.

660
00:21:34,886 --> 00:21:36,438
And income growth,

661
00:21:36,438 --> 00:21:38,938
step nine, matters even more.

662
00:21:39,288 --> 00:21:40,194
Question two.

663
00:21:40,374 --> 00:21:41,779
Should I buy a house?

664
00:21:41,959 --> 00:21:43,694
A home can be a great choice

665
00:21:43,694 --> 00:21:45,429
if you plan to stay for many

666
00:21:45,429 --> 00:21:47,474
years and the full cost of owning

667
00:21:47,474 --> 00:21:49,705
fits comfortably within your budget,

668
00:21:49,705 --> 00:21:51,688
with your emergency fund intact.

669
00:21:51,868 --> 00:21:53,296
But don't stretch for one.

670
00:21:53,476 --> 00:21:56,638
Renting while investing consistently is also a

671
00:21:56,638 --> 00:21:58,700
perfectly good path to wealth.

672
00:21:59,050 --> 00:22:00,037
Question three.

673
00:22:00,217 --> 00:22:01,947
Is it worth having a side hustle?

674
00:22:02,127 --> 00:22:02,802
It can be,

675
00:22:02,802 --> 00:22:04,893
especially for a specific goal,

676
00:22:04,893 --> 00:22:07,862
like clearing debt or building the emergency

677
00:22:07,862 --> 00:22:08,199
fund.

678
00:22:08,379 --> 00:22:10,596
But protect your time and health,

679
00:22:10,596 --> 00:22:12,812
and send side income to the plan,

680
00:22:12,812 --> 00:22:14,625
not to new lifestyle costs.

681
00:22:14,805 --> 00:22:16,981
A side hustle that funds a bigger

682
00:22:16,981 --> 00:22:18,695
car payment hasn't helped.

683
00:22:19,044 --> 00:22:19,973
Question four.

684
00:22:20,153 --> 00:22:21,326
What about kids?

685
00:22:21,506 --> 00:22:22,992
Kids add real costs,

686
00:22:22,992 --> 00:22:24,478
and the plan adapts.

687
00:22:24,658 --> 00:22:27,063
Many families lower their savings rate for

688
00:22:27,063 --> 00:22:27,750
a few years,

689
00:22:27,750 --> 00:22:28,896
then raise it again.

690
00:22:29,076 --> 00:22:30,901
Keep at least the match going,

691
00:22:30,901 --> 00:22:32,543
protect the emergency fund,

692
00:22:32,543 --> 00:22:35,341
and remember that your retirement comes before

693
00:22:35,341 --> 00:22:36,497
their college fund,

694
00:22:36,497 --> 00:22:38,809
because they can borrow for school and

695
00:22:38,809 --> 00:22:40,755
you can't borrow for retirement.

696
00:22:41,105 --> 00:22:42,290
And question five.

697
00:22:42,470 --> 00:22:43,740
How do I know if I'm on

698
00:22:43,740 --> 00:22:44,072
track?

699
00:22:44,252 --> 00:22:46,992
A rough guideline many planners use is

700
00:22:46,992 --> 00:22:49,516
to have about one times your salary

701
00:22:49,516 --> 00:22:50,669
saved by thirty,

702
00:22:50,669 --> 00:22:52,184
three times by forty,

703
00:22:52,184 --> 00:22:53,554
six times by fifty,

704
00:22:53,554 --> 00:22:56,222
and eight to ten times by retirement.

705
00:22:56,402 --> 00:22:57,934
These are rough targets,

706
00:22:57,934 --> 00:22:58,573
not rules.

707
00:22:58,753 --> 00:22:59,771
If you're behind,

708
00:22:59,771 --> 00:23:01,028
the plan still works.

709
00:23:01,208 --> 00:23:03,489
You just lean harder on steps seven

710
00:23:03,489 --> 00:23:04,076
and nine.

711
00:23:04,425 --> 00:23:07,492
It's worth remembering how new this opportunity

712
00:23:07,492 --> 00:23:07,688
is.

713
00:23:07,868 --> 00:23:09,072
For most of history,

714
00:23:09,072 --> 00:23:11,659
investing in the stock market was something

715
00:23:11,659 --> 00:23:13,104
only wealthy people did.

716
00:23:13,284 --> 00:23:15,743
Buying shares meant paying a broker large

717
00:23:15,743 --> 00:23:19,222
commissions, and building a diversified portfolio required

718
00:23:19,222 --> 00:23:20,122
a lot of money.

719
00:23:20,472 --> 00:23:22,429
That started to change in the second

720
00:23:22,429 --> 00:23:24,060
half of the twentieth century.

721
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:26,946
In 1974, Congress passed ERISA,

722
00:23:26,946 --> 00:23:30,960
the law that created the individual retirement

723
00:23:30,960 --> 00:23:31,659
account.

724
00:23:31,839 --> 00:23:33,333
In 1976, Vanguard,

725
00:23:33,333 --> 00:23:35,158
founded by John Bogle,

726
00:23:35,158 --> 00:23:38,312
launched one of the first index mutual

727
00:23:38,312 --> 00:23:40,719
funds for ordinary investors,

728
00:23:40,719 --> 00:23:43,873
letting people own a slice of hundreds

729
00:23:43,873 --> 00:23:46,363
of companies at very low cost.

730
00:23:46,712 --> 00:23:48,233
Then in 1978,

731
00:23:48,233 --> 00:23:51,509
a provision in the tax code,

732
00:23:51,509 --> 00:23:54,317
section 401k, was added.

733
00:23:54,497 --> 00:23:55,709
Within a few years,

734
00:23:55,709 --> 00:23:58,198
employers began using it to let workers

735
00:23:58,198 --> 00:24:01,389
save for retirement directly from their paychecks,

736
00:24:01,389 --> 00:24:04,516
before taxes, often with a matching contribution.

737
00:24:04,696 --> 00:24:07,535
In 1997, the Roth IRA followed,

738
00:24:07,535 --> 00:24:11,106
allowing tax free growth for people who

739
00:24:11,106 --> 00:24:12,022
qualified.

740
00:24:12,372 --> 00:24:14,884
Today, an average worker with a phone

741
00:24:14,884 --> 00:24:16,988
can open an account in minutes,

742
00:24:16,988 --> 00:24:19,160
buy a broad index fund with very

743
00:24:19,160 --> 00:24:22,554
low fees, and automate contributions every payday.

744
00:24:22,734 --> 00:24:24,868
The tools that once belonged to the

745
00:24:24,868 --> 00:24:27,367
wealthy are available to almost everyone.

746
00:24:27,547 --> 00:24:29,822
The question is no longer access.

747
00:24:30,002 --> 00:24:31,198
It's whether we use them.

748
00:24:31,548 --> 00:24:33,866
People often ask me where each dollar

749
00:24:33,866 --> 00:24:34,869
should go first.

750
00:24:35,049 --> 00:24:37,802
Here's a common order many planners suggest,

751
00:24:37,802 --> 00:24:39,867
for someone on an average salary.

752
00:24:40,217 --> 00:24:42,663
First, your workplace plan up to the

753
00:24:42,663 --> 00:24:43,410
full match.

754
00:24:43,590 --> 00:24:45,589
That's an instant return you won't find

755
00:24:45,589 --> 00:24:46,307
anywhere else.

756
00:24:46,487 --> 00:24:48,836
Second, if you qualify and have a

757
00:24:48,836 --> 00:24:50,830
high deductible health plan,

758
00:24:50,830 --> 00:24:53,535
a health savings account can offer tax

759
00:24:53,535 --> 00:24:54,817
benefits going in,

760
00:24:54,817 --> 00:24:57,878
while growing, and coming out for qualified

761
00:24:57,878 --> 00:24:58,875
medical costs.

762
00:24:59,224 --> 00:25:02,077
Third, a Roth IRA or traditional IRA,

763
00:25:02,077 --> 00:25:04,159
up to the annual IRS limit,

764
00:25:04,159 --> 00:25:07,088
which gives you more control over your

765
00:25:07,088 --> 00:25:10,481
investment choices and often lower fees than

766
00:25:10,481 --> 00:25:12,100
some workplace plans.

767
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:14,884
Fourth, go back to your workplace plan

768
00:25:14,884 --> 00:25:18,241
and raise contributions toward fifteen percent or

769
00:25:18,241 --> 00:25:18,584
more.

770
00:25:18,934 --> 00:25:21,311
And fifth, once those are on track,

771
00:25:21,311 --> 00:25:24,367
a regular taxable brokerage account for goals

772
00:25:24,367 --> 00:25:25,929
that aren't retirement,

773
00:25:25,929 --> 00:25:27,491
or for extra investing.

774
00:25:27,671 --> 00:25:29,736
The order can change based on your

775
00:25:29,736 --> 00:25:32,043
plan's fees, your tax bracket and your

776
00:25:32,043 --> 00:25:32,408
goals.

777
00:25:32,588 --> 00:25:34,572
But the principle is simple:

778
00:25:34,572 --> 00:25:36,131
take free money first,

779
00:25:36,131 --> 00:25:37,548
then tax advantages,

780
00:25:37,548 --> 00:25:38,753
then flexibility.

781
00:25:39,102 --> 00:25:39,903
One more tip.

782
00:25:40,083 --> 00:25:41,152
Keep it simple.

783
00:25:41,332 --> 00:25:42,663
On an average salary,

784
00:25:42,663 --> 00:25:45,073
you don't need ten accounts and thirty

785
00:25:45,073 --> 00:25:45,453
funds.

786
00:25:45,633 --> 00:25:47,218
Two or three accounts,

787
00:25:47,218 --> 00:25:49,380
each with one or two broad low

788
00:25:49,380 --> 00:25:52,262
cost funds, is a perfectly strong setup.

789
00:25:52,442 --> 00:25:54,610
Simple plans are easier to stick with,

790
00:25:54,610 --> 00:25:56,606
and sticking with it is what builds

791
00:25:56,606 --> 00:25:57,005
wealth.

792
00:25:57,355 --> 00:25:59,299
Let's put the twelve steps on a

793
00:25:59,299 --> 00:25:59,863
timeline.

794
00:26:00,043 --> 00:26:02,335
Here's what the plan can look like

795
00:26:02,335 --> 00:26:03,750
on an average salary,

796
00:26:03,750 --> 00:26:04,896
decade by decade.

797
00:26:05,246 --> 00:26:06,245
Your twenties.

798
00:26:06,425 --> 00:26:08,642
This is the decade of foundations.

799
00:26:08,822 --> 00:26:11,465
Steps one through four usually happen here:

800
00:26:11,465 --> 00:26:12,572
know your numbers,

801
00:26:12,572 --> 00:26:14,170
build the starter cushion,

802
00:26:14,170 --> 00:26:15,399
take the full match,

803
00:26:15,399 --> 00:26:17,182
and clear high interest debt.

804
00:26:17,362 --> 00:26:19,605
Your income is probably at its lowest,

805
00:26:19,605 --> 00:26:21,671
but every dollar you invest now has

806
00:26:21,671 --> 00:26:22,969
the most time to grow.

807
00:26:23,149 --> 00:26:25,463
A dollar invested at twenty five,

808
00:26:25,463 --> 00:26:26,655
at seven percent,

809
00:26:26,655 --> 00:26:29,811
could become roughly fifteen dollars by sixty

810
00:26:29,811 --> 00:26:30,162
five.

811
00:26:30,511 --> 00:26:32,440
The big risks in your twenties are

812
00:26:32,440 --> 00:26:33,518
not market crashes.

813
00:26:33,698 --> 00:26:36,430
They're lifestyle decisions that lock in high

814
00:26:36,430 --> 00:26:37,158
fixed costs.

815
00:26:37,338 --> 00:26:38,838
An expensive car loan,

816
00:26:38,838 --> 00:26:41,430
an apartment that takes half your take

817
00:26:41,430 --> 00:26:44,021
home pay, or credit card balances that

818
00:26:44,021 --> 00:26:45,454
follow you for years.

819
00:26:45,634 --> 00:26:47,275
Keep your fixed costs low,

820
00:26:47,275 --> 00:26:49,233
and you keep your options open.

821
00:26:49,582 --> 00:26:50,418
Your thirties.

822
00:26:50,598 --> 00:26:52,758
This is often the decade of big

823
00:26:52,758 --> 00:26:53,663
life changes.

824
00:26:53,843 --> 00:26:56,038
Partners, kids, maybe a home.

825
00:26:56,218 --> 00:26:58,680
It's also when many people see their

826
00:26:58,680 --> 00:26:59,979
first major raises.

827
00:27:00,159 --> 00:27:02,311
The key move here is step nine's

828
00:27:02,311 --> 00:27:03,050
raise rule.

829
00:27:03,230 --> 00:27:05,069
Send half of every raise to your

830
00:27:05,069 --> 00:27:07,253
future, and let the other half improve

831
00:27:07,253 --> 00:27:07,828
your life.

832
00:27:08,008 --> 00:27:10,397
Your savings rate rises without you ever

833
00:27:10,397 --> 00:27:11,293
feeling poorer.

834
00:27:11,643 --> 00:27:12,760
In your thirties,

835
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:15,191
the emergency fund matters even more,

836
00:27:15,191 --> 00:27:17,425
because more people depend on you.

837
00:27:17,605 --> 00:27:19,542
And protection, step ten,

838
00:27:19,542 --> 00:27:20,937
becomes essential.

839
00:27:21,117 --> 00:27:24,168
Term life insurance and disability insurance are

840
00:27:24,168 --> 00:27:25,821
usually cheap at this age,

841
00:27:25,821 --> 00:27:28,617
and they protect everything you're building.

842
00:27:28,967 --> 00:27:29,827
Your forties.

843
00:27:30,007 --> 00:27:32,087
This is often when the curve starts

844
00:27:32,087 --> 00:27:32,979
to bend upward.

845
00:27:33,159 --> 00:27:35,050
If you've been investing steadily,

846
00:27:35,050 --> 00:27:37,386
you may notice your investments growing by

847
00:27:37,386 --> 00:27:38,944
more in a good year than you

848
00:27:38,944 --> 00:27:39,556
contribute.

849
00:27:39,736 --> 00:27:41,501
That's compounding becoming visible.

850
00:27:41,681 --> 00:27:44,070
It's also the decade of peak expenses

851
00:27:44,070 --> 00:27:45,232
for many families,

852
00:27:45,232 --> 00:27:47,879
so the challenge is to keep contributions

853
00:27:47,879 --> 00:27:49,041
steady through it.

854
00:27:49,391 --> 00:27:50,447
If you started late,

855
00:27:50,447 --> 00:27:52,295
your forties are a powerful time to

856
00:27:52,295 --> 00:27:52,770
catch up.

857
00:27:52,950 --> 00:27:55,144
Your income is often near its peak.

858
00:27:55,324 --> 00:27:57,982
Pushing your savings rate to twenty percent

859
00:27:57,982 --> 00:28:00,146
or more for fifteen to twenty years

860
00:28:00,146 --> 00:28:02,371
can still build a strong retirement,

861
00:28:02,371 --> 00:28:04,473
even from a modest starting point.

862
00:28:04,822 --> 00:28:07,086
Your fifties and early sixties.

863
00:28:07,266 --> 00:28:08,756
Under IRS rules,

864
00:28:08,756 --> 00:28:12,108
people aged fifty and older can make

865
00:28:12,108 --> 00:28:16,018
extra catch up contributions to 401k plans

866
00:28:16,018 --> 00:28:16,856
and IRAs.

867
00:28:17,036 --> 00:28:19,389
Many average earners use this decade to

868
00:28:19,389 --> 00:28:21,682
make their biggest contributions ever,

869
00:28:21,682 --> 00:28:24,095
as kids leave home and mortgages shrink.

870
00:28:24,275 --> 00:28:26,620
This is also when you start planning

871
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:29,617
the transition: when to claim Social Security,

872
00:28:29,617 --> 00:28:30,659
how to withdraw,

873
00:28:30,659 --> 00:28:32,808
and how to reduce risk gradually.

874
00:28:33,158 --> 00:28:34,226
And at retirement,

875
00:28:34,226 --> 00:28:36,599
the average earner who followed the plan

876
00:28:36,599 --> 00:28:38,615
often has three sources of income.

877
00:28:38,795 --> 00:28:41,940
Social Security, which is designed to replace

878
00:28:41,940 --> 00:28:44,317
a larger share of income for lower

879
00:28:44,317 --> 00:28:45,645
and middle earners.

880
00:28:45,825 --> 00:28:47,926
Withdrawals from their investments.

881
00:28:48,106 --> 00:28:49,172
And, for some,

882
00:28:49,172 --> 00:28:51,609
a pension or part time work they

883
00:28:51,609 --> 00:28:52,065
enjoy.

884
00:28:52,245 --> 00:28:54,856
Together, these can provide a comfortable,

885
00:28:54,856 --> 00:28:56,286
independent retirement.

886
00:28:56,635 --> 00:28:58,757
Now, whenever I share this plan,

887
00:28:58,757 --> 00:29:00,548
I hear the same objections.

888
00:29:00,728 --> 00:29:02,261
Let's go through them honestly.

889
00:29:02,610 --> 00:29:03,655
Objection one.

890
00:29:03,835 --> 00:29:05,403
Prices are too high.

891
00:29:05,583 --> 00:29:09,048
Rent, groceries and childcare have risen fast,

892
00:29:09,048 --> 00:29:10,554
and for many people,

893
00:29:10,554 --> 00:29:12,061
the squeeze is real.

894
00:29:12,241 --> 00:29:15,212
If saving fifteen percent is impossible right

895
00:29:15,212 --> 00:29:16,862
now, don't skip the plan.

896
00:29:17,042 --> 00:29:18,980
Start with the match and the starter

897
00:29:18,980 --> 00:29:19,410
cushion.

898
00:29:19,590 --> 00:29:21,728
Then use steps five and nine,

899
00:29:21,728 --> 00:29:24,087
